---
import Figure from "./Figure.astro";
import MotionFigure from "./MotionFigure.astro";
import { localPath } from "../lib/paths";
const related = [
  { number: "01", verb: "Sense", question: "What is beneath the surface?", title: "Subsurface mapping", year: "2023 · Automation in Construction", image: "images/story/subsurface.jpg", alt: "Ground-penetrating radar and underground utility mapping research", text: "GPR inversion and vision-based mapping locate buried utilities.", output: "Site information", href: "https://wangmmstar.github.io/projects/subsurface/" },
  { number: "02", verb: "Specify", question: "Where should the work happen?", title: "BIM-to-workface guidance", year: "2025 · Automation in Construction", image: "images/story/bim-projection.png", alt: "A physical laser projection of a BIM component outline on a wall", text: "BIM-derived laser projections bring layout targets onto physical surfaces.", output: "A spatial target", href: "https://wangmmstar.github.io/projects/bim-laser/" },
  { number: "03", verb: "Act", question: "How should the robot move?", title: "Robotic Excavator", year: "2024 · This research", image: "animations/contour-map-goal.jpg", alt: "The laboratory robot excavating in a sandbox", text: "Operator demonstrations become keyframes for learning excavation motions.", output: "A learned action", href: "#method", current: true },
  { number: "04", verb: "Review", question: "What happened on site?", title: "Walkscribe", year: "Product demonstration", image: "construction-agent/inspection-evidence.jpg", alt: "Walkscribe inspection evidence from the product demonstration", text: "Field observations support guidance, review, and project records.", output: "Field evidence", href: localPath("walkscribe/") },
];
---
<section id="method" class="story-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <div><p class="story-eyebrow">01 / From a recording to a skill</p><h2>Learn the dig.<br />Leave the detours.</h2></div>
    <p>The useful lesson is a small set of essential poses. These <strong>keyframes</strong> give the robot subgoals; a learned policy connects them into a motion.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="keyframe-layout">
    <div class="keyframe-visual">
      <div class="figure-overline"><span>A demonstrated motion</span><span>The moments worth keeping</span></div>
      <a class="figure-link" data-zoom href={localPath("images/keyframe-extraction.png")} aria-label="Enlarge the keyframe extraction figure">
        <img src={localPath("images/keyframe-extraction.png")} alt="Actual paper figure: a robot digging trajectory and selected keyframes, marked by red dots among the trajectory samples." width="1397" height="611" loading="lazy" />
        <span class="enlarge-hint">↗ Enlarge</span>
      </a>
      <p class="visual-caption">Red dots mark the selected keyframes. <a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=20">Fig. 3 ↗</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="keyframe-finding"><span class="big-number">&gt;80<span>%</span></span><p>of redundant frames removed</p><span class="small-note">Reported keyframe-extraction result. <a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=32">Source ↗</a></span></div>
  </div>
  <ol class="learning-flow" aria-label="How a demonstration becomes a robot skill">
    <li><span class="step-index">01</span><div><h3>Demonstrate</h3><p>An operator provides the trajectory.</p></div></li>
    <li><span class="step-index">02</span><div><h3>Select</h3><p>Extract poses that guide the task.</p></div></li>
    <li><span class="step-index">03</span><div><h3>Learn</h3><p>Train the actions between subgoals.</p></div></li>
  </ol>
  <details class="research-detail">
    <summary>How keyframe selection and policy learning fit together</summary>
    <Figure file="method-framework.png" alt="Keyframe identification, visual representation, goal generation, and hierarchical policy learning in the proposed framework." width={1430} height={629} paperFigure={1} page={12}>The high-level policy proposes subgoals; a low-level Soft Actor-Critic policy learns how to reach them.</Figure>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="results" class="story-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <div><p class="story-eyebrow">02 / From a skill to an action</p><h2>A robot needs to know<br />what success looks like.</h2></div>
    <p>Give it a target contour—or let a visual model generate a goal from the observed scene. The learned policy turns that goal into excavation.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="evidence-layout">
    <div class="results-demo" data-tabs>
      <div class="story-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Excavation goal formulation">
        <button type="button" id="tab-contour" role="tab" aria-controls="result-contour" aria-selected="true">Specify the goal</button>
        <button type="button" id="tab-vision" role="tab" aria-controls="result-vision" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Generate it from vision</button>
      </div>
      <div id="result-contour" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-contour" tabindex="0"><MotionFigure file="contour-map-goal" title="Excavate toward a target contour">A specified site contour defines the goal for the motion.</MotionFigure></div>
      <div id="result-vision" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-vision" tabindex="0" hidden><MotionFigure file="visual-latent-goal" title="Excavate toward a generated visual goal" autoplay={false}>A learned model generates the target representation from visual observations.</MotionFigure></div>
      <p class="media-note">Actual laboratory executions · Separate demonstrations</p>
    </div>
    <div class="evidence-finding">
      <p class="mini-label">Why connect vision and action?</p>
      <div class="result-number"><span>40%</span><span class="result-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>&gt;60%</strong></div>
      <p>In the representation study, excavation success improved when visual features were coupled with robot actions, compared with a pure autoencoder.</p>
      <a class="small-source" href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=33">Visual representation study ↗</a>
      <div class="evidence-boundary"><strong>Tested in simulation + the laboratory</strong><p>Full-size excavators and changing field conditions are the next validation step.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <details class="research-detail">
    <summary>See the comparisons, visual-goal model, and testbed</summary>
    <p class="detail-intro">The policy comparison used over 10,000 simulation trials per method. The proposed policy achieved the highest success rate among the compared methods in both goal configurations.</p>
    <Figure file="policy-results.png" alt="Task-success curves for imitation-learning baselines and the proposed policy under specified and visual latent goals." width={1371} height={607} paperFigure={8} page={28}>The visual-goal formulation trades a small success-rate reduction for automatic goal generation. The original left panel offsets the behavior-cloning curve by −0.01 for visibility.</Figure>
    <Figure file="latent-goal-generation.png" alt="The encoder and goal-generation network predict a target in latent space from the observed scene." width={1431} height={369} paperFigure={4} page={22}>Visual observations are connected with robot states for goal generation.</Figure>
    <div class="testbed-detail"><Figure file="paper-testbed.png" alt="Robot arm, sandbox, camera, and HTC VIVE controllers used in the laboratory." width={701} height={743} paperFigure={6} page={24}>A controlled testbed for learning excavation.</Figure><div><h3>An operator is the starting point.</h3><p>HTC VIVE controllers capture demonstrations. A depth camera observes the sandbox. Robot arms let the team study learning before adapting it to full-size hydraulic equipment.</p><p>Demonstration quality, unfamiliar conditions, and limited real-world data remain open challenges.</p></div></div>
  </details>
</section>

<section id="field" class="story-section deployment-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <div><p class="story-eyebrow">03 / From a laboratory skill to useful work</p><h2>Start with one machine.<br />One repeatable task.<br />An operator in control.</h2></div>
    <p>Imagine a supervised pilot for a bounded digging task. An experienced operator teaches it; the system learns it; the crew decides when it is ready to repeat.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="deployment" data-tabs data-deployment>
    <figure class="deployment-visual">
      <img src={localPath("images/story/deployment-concept.webp")} alt="AI-generated deployment concept: a full-size excavator with a sensor pod in a marked work area, supervised from a separate tablet station." width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" />
      <span class="concept-tag">Future deployment concept · AI illustration</span>
      <button type="button" class="scene-point point-machine is-active" data-point="machine" aria-label="Explore the machine" aria-controls="pilot-machine">1</button>
      <button type="button" class="scene-point point-operator" data-point="operator" aria-label="Explore the operator" aria-controls="pilot-operator">2</button>
      <button type="button" class="scene-point point-task" data-point="task" aria-label="Explore the task" aria-controls="pilot-task">3</button>
    </figure>
    <div class="deployment-controls">
      <div class="deployment-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Explore the proposed field pilot">
        <button type="button" id="tab-machine" data-step="machine" role="tab" aria-controls="pilot-machine" aria-selected="true"><span>1</span> The machine</button>
        <button type="button" id="tab-operator" data-step="operator" role="tab" aria-controls="pilot-operator" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>2</span> The operator</button>
        <button type="button" id="tab-task" data-step="task" role="tab" aria-controls="pilot-task" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1"><span>3</span> The task</button>
      </div>
      <div class="deployment-panel">
        <div id="pilot-machine" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-machine" tabindex="0"><h3>Build around equipment crews already use.</h3><p>A proposed retrofit would connect cameras, machine state, and control interfaces to the learning system. Hydraulic response and sensing would need validation on the actual machine.</p><span class="pilot-question">First test: can the learned motion transfer to real equipment?</span></div>
        <div id="pilot-operator" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-operator" tabindex="0" hidden><h3>Make expertise part of the workflow.</h3><p>The operator demonstrates, reviews trial runs, and takes over when needed. Useful interventions can become new training examples after review and offline validation.</p><span class="pilot-question">First test: can a crew teach and supervise the task with reasonable effort?</span></div>
        <div id="pilot-task" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-task" tabindex="0" hidden><h3>Earn autonomy one work cycle at a time.</h3><p>Begin inside a defined work zone, with a bounded digging motion and a checked target. Expand to new materials or conditions only after evaluating completed work and interventions.</p><span class="pilot-question">First test: can the machine repeat useful work at the required quality?</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="pilot-workflow">
    <div><span class="mini-label">Teach</span><h3>Demonstrate a bounded dig</h3><p>Capture the operator’s motion and the scene.</p></div>
    <div><span class="mini-label">Validate</span><h3>Try it under supervision</h3><p>Check the result. Review takeovers. Refine offline.</p></div>
    <div><span class="mini-label">Repeat</span><h3>Measure completed work</h3><p>Track material moved, cycle time, quality, and interventions.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="field-bottom"><p><strong>The product question:</strong> can one operator teach a useful skill once, then supervise it across repeated work?</p><a href="mailto:shuai.li@ufl.edu?subject=Robotic%20Excavator%20research%20pilot">Discuss a research pilot ↗</a></div>
</section>

<section id="bigger-picture" class="story-section connected-section">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <div><p class="story-eyebrow">04 / From one useful dig to a connected site</p><h2>Another brick<br />in the wall.</h2></div>
    <p>A machine also needs context, a task, and feedback from the work. Earlier and later projects involving the team address these complementary parts.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="research-chain">
    {related.map((item) => (
      <article class:list={["chain-project", { "chain-current": item.current }]}>
        <div class="chain-stage"><span>{item.number}</span><h3>{item.verb}</h3><span class="chain-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></div>
        <p class="chain-question">{item.question}</p>
        <a class="chain-image" href={item.href} aria-label={`Explore ${item.title}`}><img src={localPath(item.image)} alt={item.alt} width="600" height="400" loading="lazy" /></a>
        <p class="chain-year">{item.year}</p>
        <h4><a href={item.href}>{item.title} <span aria-hidden="true">{item.current ? "↑" : "↗"}</span></a></h4>
        <p class="chain-description">{item.text}</p>
        <p class="chain-output">{item.output}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div class="integration-note"><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span><p><strong>The next opportunity is to connect these capabilities.</strong> Site information informs a task; a skill carries it out; field evidence informs the next decision. This is a proposed integration across projects, not an already deployed system.</p></div>
  <div class="closing-thought"><p>It starts with a small question.</p><h2>Which part of this dig<br />is worth learning?</h2><a href="#overview">Watch the demonstration again ↑</a></div>
</section>
